## DESCRIPTION
## A problem that asks students to find the margin of error and confidence interval for
## the size of a 16-ounce soda.
## ENDDESCRIPTION

# This problem is derived from a homework problem in Introductory Statistics,
# licensed by OpenStax under a Creative Commons Attribution License (CC BY 4.0).
# Modified for WeBWorK by Michael Stassen   mstassen(at)fitchburgstate(dot)edu

## DBsubject('Introductory Statistics')
## DBchapter('7')
## DBsection('1')
## KEYWORDS('confidence interval', 'margin of error')
## Author('Michael Stassen')
## Institution('Fitchburg State University')

DOCUMENT();
loadMacros('PGstandard.pl', "PGML.pl", "PGstatisticsmacros.pl", 'PGcourse.pl');

Context("LimitedNumeric");
loadMacros("fixedPrecision.pl");    # must come after Context is set.

$conf  = list_random(90, 95, 99);
$alpha = 1 - $conf / 100;

$n    = random(30,    40,    1);
$Xbar = random(13.31, 14.29, .02);
$s    = random(1.45,  1.65,  .02);

$t = round(tdistr($n - 1, $alpha / 2), 3);

$E = FixedPrecision($t * $s / sqrt($n), 2);

$X1 = FixedPrecision($Xbar - $E, 2);
$X2 = FixedPrecision($Xbar + $E, 2);

BEGIN_PGML
A quality control specialist for a restaurant chain takes a random sample of
size [$n] to check the amount of soda served in the 16 oz. serving size, finding
a mean of [$Xbar] oz. with a standard deviation of [$s] oz.

We wish to calculate a [$conf]% confidence interval for the mean amount of soda
served in the 16 oz. serving size. (Round to the nearest 0.01 oz.) 

a. What is the margin of error for a [$conf]% confidence interval? [____]{$E} oz.  
b. The [$conf]% confidence interval is from [____]{$X1} oz. to [____]{$X2} oz.  

END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
For this problem, the population standard deviation is *not* known so the [`t`]-distribution is used, however, because the samples size is fairly large, using the [`z`]-distribution should be nearly the same answer. 

a) The margin of error for this is 
[``` ME = t_{\alpha/2,n} \frac{s}{\sqrt{n}} = [$t] \frac{[$s]}{\sqrt{[$n]}} = [$E] ```]

b) The confidence interval is from [`\bar{x}-ME`] to [`\bar{x}+ME`] or [`[$X1]`] to [`[$X2]`].
END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();